														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4847482">
									

<p>Here are some minor quibbles with the internal logic you've presented:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>causing part of it to become corporal before the unit is destroyed, causing part of the earths core to collapse in on itself, creating a minor earthquake.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>Several things with this: <strong>a)</strong> If something was incorporeal in the core and then became corporeal, why would the core collapse? The core isn't hollow, its solid with liquified rock. Also, the core is made the way it is because of gravity pushing innumerable metric tons of matter into it, I can't imagine a little more would suddenly make it collapse even a teensy bit. More than likely that matter would then be vaporized by the intense heat and then some small amount of gas would escape the core potentially (but this is happening all the time). <strong>b)</strong> if part of the core were to collapse it wouldn't make a &quot;minor earthquake&quot; it would probably be disastrous.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>The exact size of this mass is [REDACTED] by it is implied to be truly massive.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>Don't do this, don't redact the size. It doesn't serve anything, do some research and figure out what sorta size you want to imply and just say it. Would be more impactful. Also in general, I am of the opinion that &quot;redacted&quot; information doesn't serve narratives in 99% of the case. Here isn't an exception to me.</em></p>
<hr /></div>
</div>
</div>
<p>More of concern is the lack of narrative. There isn't an arc to this, its merely discussing the description of an anomaly. What is it you want to say with this article? What do you want the readers to take away from it? We can't really know what the people are going through as they sink to the center of the earth&#8230; so where is the human aspect of this anomaly?</p>
<p>I think you should brainstorm concerning a narrative. Think about what sort of story you want to tell and come up with a way to tell it with this anomaly. Because at the end of the day, most articles that only concern themselves with &quot;look at this weird phenomenon&quot; don't do well. If you have some clever way to avoid using narrative to getting the reader engaged, I am not seeing it.</p>

							</div>

								
					
